The code statements that lead to the bug are:
if (WARN_ON_ONCE(!rwb))
if (WARN_ON_ONCE(wbt_init(disk, rwb)))
Reason:
The WARN_ON_ONCE macro is used to detect bugs, but these failure paths (memory allocation failure and -EBUSY) are expected under normal conditions such as memory pressure or duplicate registration, causing spurious stack traces and false positives.

The code statements that fix the bug are:
if (!rwb)
if (wbt_init(disk, rwb)) {
	pr_warn("%s: failed to enable wbt\n", disk->disk_name);
	wbt_free(rwb);
	return;
}
Reason:
Replacing WARN_ON_ONCE with plain if-checks avoids triggering unnecessary warnings for expected failures, and adding a pr_warn() retains diagnostic information without a full stack trace.

